\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSInvocation is an Objective-C message rendered static, an action turned into an object. NSInvocation objects are used to store and forward messages between objects and between applications, primarily by NSTimers and the distributed objects system. An NSInvocation contains all the elements of an Objective-C message: a target, a selector, arguments, and the return value. Each of these elements can be set directly, and the return value is set automatically when the NSInvocation is dispatched.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSInvocation can be repeatedly dispatched to different targets; its arguments can be modified between dispatch for varying results; even its selector can be changed to another with the same method signature (argument and return types). This makes it useful for repeating messages with many arguments and variations; rather than retyping a slightly different expression for each message, you modify the NSInvocation as needed each time before dispatching it to a new target.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Creating an NSInvocation requires several steps. Consider this method of the custom class MyCalendar:\
\fs14\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b updateAppointmentsForDate:\b0 (NSDate\'80*)\i aDate\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \i0 \fs28 \b updateAppointmentsForDate:\b0  takes an NSDate as its only argument, and returns YES or NO depending on whether the appointments could be updated without conflicts. The following code fragment sets up an NSInvocation for it:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 SEL theSelector;\
\fi0 NSMethodSignature *aSignature;\
\fi0 NSInvocation *anInvocation;\
\fi0 \
theSelector = @selector(updateAppointmentsForDate:);\
\fi0 aSignature = [MyCalendar methodSignatureForSelector:theSelector]\
\fi0 anInvocation = [NSInvocation invocationWithMethodSignature:aSignature];\
\fi0 [anInvocation setSelector:theSelector];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The first two lines get the NSMethodSignature for the \b updateAppointmentsForDate:\b0  method. The last two lines actually create the NSInvocation and set its selector. Note that the selector can be set to any selector matching the signature of \b updateAppointmentsForDate:\b0 . Any of these methods can be used with \b anInvocation\b0 :\
\fs14\
\pard \s4 \li2338 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2338 \tx3753 \tx4146 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b clearAppointmentsForDate:\b0 (NSDate\'80*)\i aDate\
\fi0 \i0 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b isAvailableOnDate:\b0 (NSDate\'80*)\i aDate\
\fi0 \i0 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b setMeetingTime:\b0 (NSDate\'80*)\i aDate\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \i0 \fs28 Before being dispatched, \b anInvocation\b0  must have its target and arguments set:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 MyCalendar *userDatebook;    /* Assume this exists. */\
\fi0 NSDate *todaysDate;          /* Assume this exists. */\
\fi0 \
[anInvocation setTarget:userDatebook];\
\fi0 [anInvocation setArgument:&todaysDate atIndex:3];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \b setArgument:atIndex:\b0  sets the specified argument to the value supplied. Every method has two hidden arguments, the target and selector, so the first argument that needs to be set is actually at index 3. In this case, \b todaysDate\b0  will be the NSDate argument to \b updateAppointmentsForDate:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 To dispatch the NSInvocation, send an \b invoke\b0  or \b invokeWithTarget:\b0  message. \b invoke\b0  only produces a result if the NSInvocation has a target set. Once dispatched, the NSInvocation contains the return value of the message, which \b getReturnValue:\b0  produces:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 BOOL result;\
\
[anInvocation invoke];\
\fi0 [anInvocation getReturnValue:&result];\
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Saving NSInvocations for Later Use\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Because an NSInvocation doesn\'27t always need to retain its arguments, by default it doesn\'27t do so. This can cause object arguments as well as the target to become invalid if they\'27re automatically released. If you plan to cache an NSInvocation or dispatch it repeatedly during the execution of your application, you should send it a \b retainArguments\b0  message. This method retains the target and all object arguments, and copies C strings so that they\'27re not lost because another object frees them.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Using NSInvocations with NSTimers\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Suppose the NSInvocation created above is being used in a time-management application that allows multiple users to set appointments for others, such as group meetings. This application might allow a user\'27s calendar to be automatically updated every few minutes, so that the user always knows what his schedule looks like. Such automatic updating can be accomplished by setting up NSTimer objects with NSInvocations.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Given the NSInvocation above, this is as simple as invoking one NSTimer method:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 [NSTimer scheduledTimerWithInterval:600\
\fi0     invocation:anInvocation\
\fi0     repeats:YES];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 This line of code sets up an NSTimer to dispatch \b anInvocation\b0  every 10 minutes (600 seconds). Note that an NSTimer always instructs its NSInvocation to retain its arguments; thus, you don\'27t need to send \b retainArguments\b0  yourself. See the NSTimer class specification for more information on timers.\
\fs14\
